# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_workflow]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=frontend, web, ui, workflow, plan, new feature, component, page, checklist, review]

::dense
Frontend workflow: read existing code, match conventions, build small, check a11y/responsive/states, test, verify.

::steps
1. Read the existing project first: framework, styling approach, folder layout, lint config, design tokens. Match them; do not introduce a new library without a reason.
2. Clarify the target: which screens, which breakpoints, which browsers, which states (loading, empty, error, success).
3. Break the UI into small components with one job each. Decide where state lives before writing markup.
4. Build semantic HTML first, then layout, then behaviour. Reach for platform features before libraries.
5. Cover every state: loading, empty, error, partial, offline, long text, and no-JS fallback where it matters.
6. Check keyboard navigation, focus order, contrast, and a 320px-wide viewport.
7. Add tests for behaviour that matters, then run lint, type-check, tests and the build.
8. Summarise what changed, what was not verified in a real browser, and any follow-ups.

::warning
Never claim a UI works visually unless it was actually rendered or tested. State what was and was not verified.

::assertions
assert task != "" "A description of the UI task is required."
assert framework != "" "The target framework or stack must be known (React, Vue, plain HTML...)."

::edges
->[:requires] frontend_html_a11y
->[:requires] frontend_css_responsive
->[:see-also] frontend_testing

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_html_a11y]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=html, semantic, accessibility, a11y, aria, wcag, keyboard, focus, forms, label, alt, screen reader, accessible, form, input, button, contrast, tab order, modal, dialog]

::dense
Semantic HTML first; keyboard-operable; labelled controls; alt text; contrast 4.5:1; ARIA only when HTML cannot do it.

::steps
1. Use the right element: button for actions, a for navigation, nav/main/header/footer/section for landmarks, ul/ol for lists, table for tabular data.
2. One h1 per page; headings descend in order without skipping levels.
3. Every form control has a visible label tied by for/id (or wrapped). Placeholders are not labels.
4. Every interactive element is reachable and operable by keyboard, with a visible focus style. Never remove outline without a replacement.
5. Images get meaningful alt text; decorative images get alt="".
6. Text contrast is at least 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text and UI components). Never use colour alone to convey meaning.
7. Use ARIA only when native HTML cannot express the pattern. Prefer aria-label, aria-live, aria-expanded over custom roles.
8. Modals trap and restore focus, close on Escape, and mark the rest of the page inert.
9. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and avoid auto-playing media.
10. Set lang on the html element and a meaningful title per page.

::code[lang=html]
<label for="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required aria-describedby="email-hint">
<p id="email-hint">We only use this for receipts.</p>
<button type="submit">Create account</button>

::warning
A div with an onClick is not a button. It breaks keyboard use, focus and screen readers. Use a real button.

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_css_responsive]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=css, layout, flexbox, grid, responsive, mobile first, breakpoints, tokens, dark mode, tailwind, spacing, style, styling, mobile, layout shift, center, grid layout, theme]

::dense
Mobile-first CSS; flex/grid layout; relative units; design tokens as variables; avoid fixed heights; test 320px to wide.

::steps
1. Design mobile-first: base styles for small screens, then add min-width media queries.
2. Use Grid for two-dimensional layout and Flexbox for one-dimensional rows or columns. Use gap, not margin hacks.
3. Define colours, spacing, radii, font sizes and shadows as CSS custom properties (design tokens) on :root. Never hard-code the same value in many places.
4. Use rem for type and spacing, % or fr for widths, and clamp() for fluid type. Avoid fixed pixel heights on text containers.
5. Support dark mode with prefers-color-scheme and tokens, not duplicated rules.
6. Keep specificity low: single class selectors, no ids, avoid !important. Use a methodology (BEM, CSS Modules, or utility classes) and stay consistent.
7. Make tap targets at least 44px, and let long words wrap (overflow-wrap).
8. Use logical properties (margin-inline, padding-block) when the product may be RTL.
9. Check the layout at 320px, 768px, 1280px and with browser zoom at 200%.

::code[lang=css]
:root { --space-2: 0.5rem; --space-4: 1rem; --color-text: #1a1a1a; --color-bg: #fff; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --color-text: #eee; --color-bg: #111; } }
.cards { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); }
h1 { font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.2rem + 2vw, 3rem); }

::note
With Tailwind or a component library, follow its tokens and config instead of writing parallel custom CSS.

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_typescript]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=javascript, typescript, types, async, errors, null, lint, eslint, prettier, modules, strict, type, promise, await, lint, error handling, js, ts]

::dense
Strict TypeScript; no any; handle async errors; small pure functions; ESLint + Prettier; ES modules.

::steps
1. Turn on strict mode. Avoid any; use unknown plus narrowing, or precise types. Model states with discriminated unions.
2. Validate data at the boundary (API responses, form input, URL params) with a schema library such as Zod, and infer types from it.
3. Always handle rejected promises. Use try/catch around await, and surface failures to the user.
4. Keep functions small and pure where possible. Separate data transformation from rendering.
5. Prefer const, immutable updates, and array methods over mutation.
6. Use named exports, ES modules, and absolute or aliased imports consistently.
7. Name things by intent (isLoading, onSubmit, userList). Avoid abbreviations.
8. Let ESLint and Prettier decide style. Never fight the formatter in review.
9. Remove dead code and console.log before finishing.

::code[lang=typescript]
type Status<T> =
  | { kind: "loading" }
  | { kind: "error"; message: string }
  | { kind: "ready"; data: T };

async function getUser(id: string, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`, { signal });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return userSchema.parse(await res.json());
}

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_react]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=react, component, hooks, props, state, effect, memo, key, composition, vue, rendering, components, hook, useeffect, usestate, props, jsx, next, nextjs]

::dense
Small function components; derive don't sync state; effects only for external sync; stable keys; compose over config.

::steps
1. Write small function components with typed props. One component, one responsibility.
2. Keep state as local as possible; lift it only when siblings need it. Derive values during render instead of copying props into state.
3. Use useEffect only to sync with external systems (subscriptions, timers, DOM APIs). Do not use it to compute values or respond to events. Always clean up.
4. Use stable, unique keys for lists, never the array index for reorderable data.
5. Prefer composition (children, slots) over many boolean props.
6. Do not memoise by default. Measure first, then use memo, useMemo or useCallback where a real cost exists.
7. Use controlled inputs consistently; keep form state in one place or a form library.
8. Wrap risky subtrees in error boundaries, and use Suspense with meaningful fallbacks.
9. Extract repeated logic into custom hooks named useSomething.
10. Follow the project's own conventions if it uses Vue, Svelte or another framework; the same principles apply.

::warning
Never mutate state or props directly, and never call hooks conditionally or inside loops.

::edges
->[:requires] frontend_typescript
->[:see-also] frontend_state_data

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_state_data]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=state, data fetching, api, cache, loading, error state, query, forms, validation, optimistic, zustand, redux, fetch, forms, loading, spinner, empty state, request, rest, graphql]

::dense
Server state via query cache; client state minimal; always loading/empty/error UI; validate forms; cancel stale requests.

::steps
1. Separate server state (remote data) from client state (UI). Use a query library (TanStack Query, SWR, RTK Query) for server state: caching, retries, dedupe, invalidation.
2. Keep client state minimal. Prefer component state, then context, then a store (Zustand, Redux) only when truly shared.
3. Every data view renders loading, empty, error and success states. Offer a retry on failure.
4. Cancel or ignore stale requests (AbortController) so slow responses cannot overwrite newer ones.
5. Validate forms on the client for usability and on the server for truth. Show errors next to fields and move focus to the first error.
6. Disable the submit button while pending to prevent double submits. Use optimistic updates only with rollback.
7. Keep URLs as state for shareable views (filters, tabs, pagination) via search params.
8. Never store sensitive tokens in localStorage; see the security node.

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_performance]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=performance, lighthouse, core web vitals, lcp, cls, inp, bundle, lazy, images, code splitting, caching, slow, fast, speed, optimize, optimise, image, load time, bundle size]

::dense
Measure first; optimise LCP, CLS, INP; code-split; sized responsive images; lazy-load; cache; trim dependencies.

::steps
1. Measure before optimising: Lighthouse, Chrome DevTools Performance, and real-user Core Web Vitals.
2. Targets: LCP under 2.5s, INP under 200ms, CLS under 0.1.
3. Images: modern formats (AVIF/WebP), srcset and sizes, explicit width and height, loading="lazy" below the fold, and fetchpriority="high" for the hero image.
4. Split code by route and lazy-load heavy components. Check the bundle with a visualiser and drop or replace large dependencies.
5. Load fonts with font-display: swap, subset them, and limit weights.
6. Avoid layout shift: reserve space for images, ads and async content.
7. Keep main-thread work short; defer or move heavy work to a Web Worker. Debounce expensive input handlers.
8. Virtualise very long lists.
9. Use HTTP caching headers and a CDN for static assets with hashed filenames.

::code[lang=html]
<img src="hero-800.avif" srcset="hero-400.avif 400w, hero-800.avif 800w, hero-1600.avif 1600w"
     sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 50vw, 100vw" width="1600" height="900" alt="Product dashboard" fetchpriority="high">

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_security]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=security, xss, csrf, csp, sanitize, cookies, token, secrets, dependencies, cors, innerhtml, secure, vulnerability, injection, auth, password, sanitise]

::dense
Escape output; never raw innerHTML; httpOnly cookies; CSP; no secrets in client; audit dependencies; HTTPS only.

::steps
1. Treat all external data as untrusted. Let the framework escape output. Avoid innerHTML and dangerouslySetInnerHTML; if unavoidable, sanitise with DOMPurify.
2. Store session tokens in httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies, not localStorage. Protect state-changing requests against CSRF.
3. Set a Content-Security-Policy and other security headers on the server.
4. Never put secrets or private API keys in client code or env vars exposed to the browser. Anything shipped to the browser is public.
5. Validate and encode URLs before navigating or embedding; reject javascript: URLs.
6. Add rel="noopener noreferrer" to links that use target="_blank".
7. Audit dependencies regularly (npm audit), pin versions with a lockfile, and avoid unmaintained packages.
8. Load third-party scripts sparingly, with Subresource Integrity where possible.
9. Do not log personal data to the console or analytics.

::warning
Client-side checks are for usability only. Authorisation and validation must be enforced on the server.

::end

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::node[frontend_testing]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=testing, unit test, vitest, jest, testing library, playwright, e2e, coverage, mock, accessibility test, test, tests, tested, unit, e2e test, mocking, flaky]

::dense
Test behaviour not implementation; Testing Library queries by role; Playwright for key flows; mock network; run in CI.

::steps
1. Test what the user sees and does, not internal state. Query by role, label or text (Testing Library), not by class names or test ids unless nothing else works.
2. Unit-test pure logic (formatters, reducers, validators) with Vitest or Jest. Component-test interactions and states.
3. Mock the network at the boundary (MSW) so tests exercise real fetch code paths.
4. Cover the sad paths: error responses, empty data, slow requests, and invalid form input.
5. Use Playwright or Cypress for a small number of critical end-to-end flows (sign in, checkout, core task).
6. Add automated accessibility checks (axe) and review keyboard flow by hand.
7. Keep tests deterministic: no real timers, random data or network. Fix flaky tests instead of retrying them.
8. Run lint, type-check, tests and the production build in CI before merge.

::code[lang=typescript]
test("shows an error when the request fails", async () => {
  server.use(http.get("/api/users", () => HttpResponse.error()));
  render(<UserList />);
  expect(await screen.findByRole("alert")).toHaveTextContent(/something went wrong/i);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /retry/i }));
});

::end
